plans.2827708e14e1a11974765e23abc54c8e:
{"text":"Yes. Several terms are used in Footnote's definitions without any explanation of their own, but they are not really unexplained: each one is a other name for a metric that is defined. Gross sales, for example, is defined as the value of every order at full price, in euros, before returns, discounts and VAT, and it is also written as GMV [@metric:gross_sales]. Average order value is defined as the mean value of a paid order after discounts, with gift-card purchases left out, and is also called Basket size [@metric:average_order_value]. Country is the same as Market, and Sales, Takings and Turnover all point to Net revenue.

The terms that do carry their own explanation are Peak season, Price promotion, Store credit, Test account and Webshop, so those are not jargon in the sense you asked about.

I found no context item that explains any of these terms, and the search for jargon-related items returned nothing. One guide does touch on Gross margin in Spain and is out of date because its definition changed since it was checked [#r4.items.0.checked.date] [itm_kcy22j54].","calls":[]}

